{
  "abstract": "Konstantopoulos, Patie and Sarkar asked whether the Riemann xi function belongs to their class of entire functions generated by spectrally negative Lévy exponents. For the actual even theta kernel, its Mellin transform $M$ determines the candidate coefficient factor $\\phi(s)=(4s-2)M(s-1)/M(s)$. We give a complete continuum certificate for positivity and strict decrease of its signed inverse, conditional on explicit analytic saddle, contour and moment-enclosure lemmas whose derivations and implementation are supplied. The method preserves cancellation in the Mellin quotient, subtracts a corrected Lambert model, and transfers the inverse to a bent contour retaining a positive exterior cut contribution and both finite horizontal joins. Analytic estimates handle $0<r\\le1/200000$ and every $r\\ge1$; interval reconstruction covers all 105 intervening rational intervals. The certificate contains 1432 winding rectangles, 337 low-frequency moment panels and 543 high-frequency panels. A portable CPU checker reconstructs all comparisons from outward-rounded public inputs in approximately 16 seconds; full enclosure-generation code accompanies it. The resulting conditional membership is in the ordinary class $B_J$, with zero linear drift and an explicit leading inverse pole. Telescoping coefficients then identify the associated KPS function with normalized xi. This does not establish the real-zero property: the KPS class contains functions with nonreal zeros. The complete analytic and evaluator argument remains an explicit independent-review obligation, and no GGC, Thorin or Riemann-hypothesis conclusion is asserted.",
